BOARD WIRING / DOWNSTREAM CONSUMER

D:20-29 selects model MSM8939 SMB1360 MTP, board-id<8 0>, i2c_4 charger@14.
msm8939.dtsi:24-27 supplies IDs239/241/263. msm8939-common.dtsi:960-977 defines
QUP4 at0x078b8000. D:30-33 gives STAT IRQ GPIO62, interrupts<62 8>, and smb_int
pinctrl. msm8939-pinctrl.dtsi:328-336 gives GPIO62 2mA/pull-up.
D:67-68: regulator-name="smb1360_otg_vreg"; qcom,otg-batt-curr-limit=<1500>.
D:83-88: qcom,hsusb-otg-mode=<3>, qcom,usbid-gpio=<&msm_gpio 110 0>, and
vbus_otg-supply=<&smb1360_otg_supply>. msm8939-pinctrl.dtsi:339-347 gives GPIO110
8mA/pull-up. This matches Sam's reported selected live DT rather than an
arbitrary other MSM8939 board variant.

Mode3 is USB_OTG; inherited control2 is OTG_PMIC_CONTROL
(include/linux/usb/msm_hsusb.h:67-89; msm8939-common.dtsi:795-796). That control
name does not mean this board's ID signal comes from a PMIC: U:5253-5285 requests
the SoC GPIO and both-edge IRQs, U:3794-3846 waits5ms and reads it. High sets ID;
low clears ID and sets A_BUS_REQ. Initial ID uses the same GPIO (U:2860-2871).

C:2904-2941 registers a voltage regulator on the CHARGER NODE ITSELF, with only
enable, disable, is_enabled operations and status-change permission. There is
no separate regulator child, parent-supply or output voltage/current constraint
here. U:1986-1991 gets "vbus_otg" for host; U:1927-1969 controls it. Mainline's
corresponding generic consumer uses "vbus" / vbus-supply, not this vendor name.

REGULATOR OPERATIONS / WHAT THEY DO NOT PROVE

Definitions C:156-158:
  CMD_CHG_REG=0x42; CMD_CHG_EN=BIT(1); CMD_OTG_EN_BIT=BIT(0).
Enable C:2843-2867: masked write0x42, mask0x01, value0x01.
Disable C:2070-2091,2870-2880: masked write0x42, mask0x01, value0x00.
Is-enabled C:2883-2895: read0x42; return bit0 state; propagate read failure.
C:570-595 serializes RMW with read_write_lock, preserving charger bit1.
C:427-458 uses SMBus byte-data transfers.

These callbacks contain NO voltage-setting operation, measured-voltage/status
readback, power-good poll, settling delay, battery/input-presence check, explicit
charging disable or input-isolation sequence. Command readback != valid output.

Optional qcom,otg-fet-present path (C:2848-2864,2074-2088) sets OTG bit first,
then FET GPIO low, then current-gain correction factor3; disable clears OTG first,
sets FET high and restores gain. GPIO request is open-drain initially high
(C:3623-3639). Selected D has neither OTG-FET property. Do not invent this route.

INITIALIZATION / LIMITS

C:4358-4372 registers regulator AFTER smb1360_hw_init, including charger and FG
configuration. Relevant sequence, not a recipe to write blindly:
- C:728-738,3594-3601: volatile-write unlock CMD_I2C_REG0x40 bit6.
- C:3485-3524,3603-3607: command shutdown handling via SHDN_CTRL_REG0x1a bits1
  (command control),2(polarity), and CMD_IL_REG0x41 bit7, respecting polarity.
- C:3611-3639: profile/gain and optional FET setup.
- C:3642-3675: charger control0x07 mask0xe0 value0, input control0x05 mask0xe0
  value0x60;0x06 AICL bit0 +20ms input-UV filter bit7 (0x81).
- C:3678-3919: battery charging/JEITA settings, UVLO, IRQs, OTG battery-current
  threshold, FG setup, charging state. Not all of this is proven necessary for
  boost, and copying it would overwrite unrelated battery policy.

D:48,70 declares rsense-10mhom and otp-rslow-cfg; C:4105-4113 disables both for
hardware-major<3. Board-id<8 0> does not establish that hardware-major value.

Battery-side OTG current threshold:
- CFG_BATT_CHG_REG0x00, mask bits4:3=0x18, shift3 (C:41-48).
- Table350,550,950,1500mA (C:279), codes0,1,2,3.
- C:3892-3906 rounds up, saturates at1500; selected1500 gives masked0x18.
- Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/smb1360-charger-fg.txt:104-106 explicitly
  describes current drawn FROM THE BATTERY in OTG mode, not USB output current.
- Failure writing the threshold is logged, then rc overwritten by the FG call
